{
    "title": "Nondisplacement of Qualified Workers Under Service Contracts; Rescission of Regulations",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 22, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-22/pdf/2025-23626.pdf",
    "summary": "The U.S. Department of Labor has officially rescinded the rule that would have required new federal service contractors to offer jobs first to the workers from the outgoing contractor. This move follows a January 20, 2025 executive order from President Trump that revoked the earlier order authorizing the rule. The change takes effect December 22, 2025.\n\nWhat\u2019s being rolled back is a policy designed to help workers keep their jobs when a federal service contract changes hands. In practice, the policy never really took hold because the government\u2019s contracting rules were never updated to put it into effect. The Labor Department says that, without the underlying authority from the executive branch, keeping the rule on the books no longer made sense.\n\nWho benefits? Federal service contractors and government contracting offices will see fewer compliance steps. The Department estimates companies will avoid tasks like inserting special clauses into subcontracts, providing specific notices to workers, and keeping detailed records tied to job offers. It also expects agencies to save staff time on oversight. All told, the Department projects first\u2011year net savings of about $39 million, with average annual savings of roughly $11 million over ten years. Many of these savings are spread across a large number of potential bidders and small businesses that register to compete for federal service work.\n\nWho could be negatively affected? Service employees working on federal contracts may lose a layer of job security when contracts turn over. Without a required priority offer, some workers could face more uncertainty during transitions, which is why labor advocates supported the prior policy. That said, because the rule never became fully operational, the immediate, on\u2011the\u2011ground impact may be modest.\n\nProcedurally, the Department skipped the usual public comment period, arguing it lacked legal authority to keep the rule in place after the executive order and that public input wouldn\u2019t change that. It also asked to discontinue related paperwork requirements and notified Congress as required for major rules. The bottom line: fewer administrative requirements for contractors and agencies, but less formal protection for continuity of employment for service workers on federal contracts.",
